(   [Problem Statement]: Compare CSS positioning schemes: static, relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky with architectural use cases.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The CSS position property defines the coordinate positioning context of elements:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. position: static \(Default\):) Tj
T*
(   The element flows naturally within the normal document stream. Properties top, right, bottom,) Tj
T*
(   left, and z-index have zero effect.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. position: relative:) Tj
T*
(   The element remains in the normal flow, but can be visually shifted using top/left/right/bottom) Tj
T*
(   relative to its original slot without affecting surrounding elements. Crucially acts as the) Tj
T*
(   positioning anchor for child absolute elements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. position: absolute:) Tj
T*
(   The element is completely extracted from the normal flow \(taking zero layout space\). It) Tj
T*
(   positions itself relative to its nearest ancestor with a position other than static \(or root) Tj
T*
(   viewport\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. position: fixed:) Tj
T*
(   Extracted from flow and anchored directly to the browser viewport coordinates. It remains) Tj
T*
(   stationary on screen during page scrolling \(used for persistent sticky navigation bars and) Tj
T*
(   modal overlays\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   5. position: sticky:) Tj
T*
(   Hybrid positioning. Acts as relative until the user scrolls past a specified threshold \(e.g.,) Tj
T*
(   top: 0\), whereupon it toggles to fixed within the confines of its parent container.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
