(   [Problem Statement]: Explain CSS Combinators \(Descendant, Child, Adjacent Sibling, General Sibling\) with code snippets and rendering rules.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Combinators define the relationship between two selectors:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Descendant Combinator \(Space - 'A B'\):) Tj
T*
(   Matches any element B that is a descendant of element A, regardless of nesting depth.) Tj
T*
(   `div p { color: blue; }` matches all `<p>` tags inside `<div>`, including inside child) Tj
T*
(   sections.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Child Combinator \(> - 'A > B'\):) Tj
T*
(   Matches only direct, immediate children of element A.) Tj
T*
(   `ul > li { list-style: square; }` matches only `<li>` elements that are direct children of) Tj
T*
(   `<ul>`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Adjacent Sibling Combinator \(+ - 'A + B'\):) Tj
T*
(   Matches element B only if it immediately follows element A and shares the same parent.) Tj
T*
(   `h2 + p { font-size: 1.2rem; }` styles only the introductory paragraph immediately beneath a) Tj
T*
(   heading.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. General Sibling Combinator \(~ - 'A ~ B'\):) Tj
T*
(   Matches any element B that follows element A anywhere as a sibling sharing the same parent.) Tj
T*
(   `h2 ~ p { color: #555; }` styles all subsequent paragraphs that follow an `<h2>`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
